[{:db/id "q-50135/selection/936d35aa5ed917fe595c9252186525aac366f297f2ba64ec29f7fcd392762696" :answer/id #uuid "6df3547a-313b-5d3c-a273-bbbff051fd3f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-50135/selection/b876510f3cbfa1525ece5b080c0b7327ca6bca4df086b980dc064adc8f336ae6" :answer/id #uuid "c0529f61-0ba3-505d-b0f7-3ebeb5477b0d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}={e}^{xy}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-50135/selection/fd31eee434b716e66671951fb59f0b688c81d87fc643714622fee9c6e53e8b43" :answer/id #uuid "ef8a33b7-344f-5788-8736-5604a7d25895" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{y}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-50135/selection/24f7010faf73c8da09268b65d0f694ceb73c21df7bec7109629bf608f4165016" :answer/id #uuid "5e33182a-aec5-575b-96ca-d5d58fd6d6f3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-50135/selection/1a21e89de45ec149e2e43b0c7b151ea45a9b80ad427fe7cbb3dcbc4b6be5f05a" :answer/id #uuid "623f5543-b4f4-535e-bc10-13dce009acfc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}={y}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-50135/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "5a49e3d5-80d5-5beb-8664-852d8076eee6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-50135/selection/936d35aa5ed917fe595c9252186525aac366f297f2ba64ec29f7fcd392762696" :answer-field/choices ["q-50135/selection/936d35aa5ed917fe595c9252186525aac366f297f2ba64ec29f7fcd392762696" "q-50135/selection/b876510f3cbfa1525ece5b080c0b7327ca6bca4df086b980dc064adc8f336ae6" "q-50135/selection/fd31eee434b716e66671951fb59f0b688c81d87fc643714622fee9c6e53e8b43" "q-50135/selection/24f7010faf73c8da09268b65d0f694ceb73c21df7bec7109629bf608f4165016" "q-50135/selection/1a21e89de45ec149e2e43b0c7b151ea45a9b80ad427fe7cbb3dcbc4b6be5f05a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-50135" :question/id #uuid "21d8bdd9-00f5-5d9f-b814-9441e0613ff1" :question/math-academy-id "q-50135" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069384/assets/65b74168f1165014df494c238dc400ab772f2c2486935ba6b78fdcbc6658b3b6.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069384/assets/3b77cdfa4958cd1892c31baf858b5f8cbe29e00e8e2a45b698a43c5ddae6198e.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the line $y=0,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on this line.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y \\\\ {y}^{′} & ={y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the line $y=0$ divide the plane into $2$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $2$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| $y>0$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ |\n\nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=y.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-50135/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "244419de-9b13-5923-b0a5-1bdc6f9b03b2"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-50135"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-50188/selection/e02686c15a6c17d96b9b4bb157f8f88bb9fea223237941952c356b782741ffd7" :answer/id #uuid "0f5e9e8f-6a4d-5a7f-90af-b25b07cbe45c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=x-y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-50188/selection/a67109c50eba73a91755c19336c23f2c3d70d2a8ed1c0bfa935325883a7e96f3" :answer/id #uuid "0eaf23b5-895f-56f9-a72e-8bdf564d18dc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=xy" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-50188/selection/421bc7bd296950364c2317c2c4d460e39b3a13d7ed830d8bf128db18db32c23f" :answer/id #uuid "0d778715-435c-5af7-a61a-762082a2c2b1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-50188/selection/b941113d2e5493d6b96f1cb98ee84fd73cf686a80874bafd5355d35a9036525b" :answer/id #uuid "b128b61a-3671-5aad-8b0b-b33bd3621e7c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=x+y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-50188/selection/5954fc08fa903bfee9577b64f1684f690ca6bca0a92020166f7c910aebee8aee" :answer/id #uuid "f5d16394-e87f-56fd-ad43-02845985acf9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=(x+1)y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-50188/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "5125d1f0-df69-5101-a6ab-329f67d3df8e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-50188/selection/421bc7bd296950364c2317c2c4d460e39b3a13d7ed830d8bf128db18db32c23f" :answer-field/choices ["q-50188/selection/e02686c15a6c17d96b9b4bb157f8f88bb9fea223237941952c356b782741ffd7" "q-50188/selection/a67109c50eba73a91755c19336c23f2c3d70d2a8ed1c0bfa935325883a7e96f3" "q-50188/selection/421bc7bd296950364c2317c2c4d460e39b3a13d7ed830d8bf128db18db32c23f" "q-50188/selection/b941113d2e5493d6b96f1cb98ee84fd73cf686a80874bafd5355d35a9036525b" "q-50188/selection/5954fc08fa903bfee9577b64f1684f690ca6bca0a92020166f7c910aebee8aee"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-50188" :question/id #uuid "3d30b528-f410-51c6-84f9-1799c3e31ee0" :question/math-academy-id "q-50188" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069384/assets/09ba55ca21ef70904948bcf6d9c0787bee8f7836a233e0d7ce689549966120cf.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069384/assets/0052e47a810dc6c82dd68ff9d0527a0b121f935d42067e063478f4f98817ffed.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=0$ and $y=0,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines. \n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =3{x}^{2}{y}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $x=0$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into $4$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $4$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| $y>0$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n\nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-50188/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "244419de-9b13-5923-b0a5-1bdc6f9b03b2"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-50188"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-50350"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069384/assets/2f577535c7e293a64139c0c68d2f09ea083041d806a634ae2d9a27055e96c00d.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=x+y+1$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{x+1}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{x-1}$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069384/assets/b8a9560870e28c751b8869e298ffabdbcc23eec720c7a1057d55032a62767183.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become vertical as $x\\to 1.$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become horizontal at $y=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation I does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=x+y+1$ does not evaluate to zero on $y=0$ (the value depends on $x$).\n\n- Equation II does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{x+1}$ evaluates to zero on $y=0,$ but \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{x+1}\\to \\frac{y}{2}<\\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 1.\n$$\n\n- Equation III gives the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{x-1}$ evaluates to zero on $y=0,$ and \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=|\\frac{y}{x-1}|\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-80703/selection/4adff6d97b972bb779b6b616e796032a40f5a3c8b1650a5184facb312f8b6f9d" :answer/id #uuid "8a520fac-e268-5245-b9a5-3e76ac57955b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=x-4y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80703/selection/40d983395aff6973eaf9278b612053fa9ef47ee0ff1d2e29bec2a3fb9cd3f8d3" :answer/id #uuid "a430c54e-9a5a-520c-8ec1-2a84dc75d7cd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=xy-4x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80703/selection/a67109c50eba73a91755c19336c23f2c3d70d2a8ed1c0bfa935325883a7e96f3" :answer/id #uuid "2c379474-7aaf-57e2-b86d-66a4e768a01f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=xy" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80703/selection/2e95c36be08052f8d599f95578a81abf3b1a15a32607ce1752d388db7cbd8bd1" :answer/id #uuid "f012c57e-b5e7-5560-9b2d-80d38c5a8a56" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=(y-4)^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80703/selection/dd0967aeeaa64804b1daa61ce56e01006da21f1d38908573c709f6343f02f91c" :answer/id #uuid "169680d7-f659-5228-9577-2e5f81edf31b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=x(y-4)^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80703/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "fd99a06d-d546-5826-a582-a0212f6e7f61" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-80703/selection/40d983395aff6973eaf9278b612053fa9ef47ee0ff1d2e29bec2a3fb9cd3f8d3" :answer-field/choices ["q-80703/selection/4adff6d97b972bb779b6b616e796032a40f5a3c8b1650a5184facb312f8b6f9d" "q-80703/selection/40d983395aff6973eaf9278b612053fa9ef47ee0ff1d2e29bec2a3fb9cd3f8d3" "q-80703/selection/a67109c50eba73a91755c19336c23f2c3d70d2a8ed1c0bfa935325883a7e96f3" "q-80703/selection/2e95c36be08052f8d599f95578a81abf3b1a15a32607ce1752d388db7cbd8bd1" "q-80703/selection/dd0967aeeaa64804b1daa61ce56e01006da21f1d38908573c709f6343f02f91c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-80703" :question/id #uuid "8e9fbf1d-5efa-58cb-a6e1-75538211843e" :question/math-academy-id "q-80703" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069384/assets/da6318e1b1b8123845993da64cea95f640b0cb67ad94bb183bbbe9e5b1bd7b89.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069384/assets/8f8ffabcc5b03b1f1b5856f4eb187137d1bf84043e47a43f6777df7b267853ec.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=0$ and $y=4,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines. \n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =xy-4x=x(y-4) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =x(y-4)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $x=0$ and $y=4$ divide the plane into $4$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $4$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| $y>4$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<4$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n\n \nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=xy-4x=x(y-4).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=xy-4x.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-80703/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1616167b-5679-5ff6-b385-3e1b59352c64"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-80703"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-120286/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "e23fa630-a3b0-52d3-853d-521ef5281c3a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120286/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "29196c44-9eaf-5de2-9449-15e09be3138d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120286/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "49483c01-dabe-5d9e-8609-577b6d11e377" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120286/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "41934154-1ca0-5719-a3b4-a7b2b9c1cc4c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120286/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "3d46a061-d0f7-5c63-a052-9c2b6f303086" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120286/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "f861bdfb-15c7-5413-b532-1ac602d9805a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-120286/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer-field/choices ["q-120286/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-120286/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-120286/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-120286/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-120286/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-120286" :question/id #uuid "300b5b75-ac3e-5a5e-8fbd-21f5c2d703aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-120286" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069384/assets/e8b573c6c2c59123de46346ccfc717fa89a98f7838e643a5e82972d753383588.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{y+1}{x}$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069384/assets/2f654bc96d27ac53dd501b10c5beb0d1c7d04ff4f643c5044692afac26ca2ba8.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- For $x\\ne 0,$ the slopes of the slope field become horizontal at $y=0$ only.\n\n- For $y\\ne 0,$ the slopes of the slope field become vertical as $x\\to 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation I gives the required behavior. For $x\\ne 0,$ the right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}$ evaluates to zero on $y=0$ only, and for $y\\ne 0,$ we have \n\n$$\n|{y}^{′}|=|-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}|\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 0.\n$$\n\n- Equation II does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}$ does not evaluate to zero on $y=0.$\n\n- Equation III does *not* give the required behavior. For $x\\ne 0,$ the right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=\\frac{y+1}{x}$ does not evaluate to zero on $y=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-120286/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3e3fdc8-67d0-5b46-9c6b-c1ec8d4ea24f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-120286"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
